= Ghee (Ghṛta)

_Ayurvedic status: fully classical and central — ghṛta is one of the most-praised substances in the saṃhitās, the foundational lipid-and-vehicle of the Ayurvedic pharmacy, and the most-cited_ saṃskāra-vehicle _(yogavāhī, "carrier of yoga / carrier of the formulation") in classical materia medica. Chinese / Tibetan: classical in the Tibetan-medical tradition (the_ Gyud Zhi _system, the Mongolian-Tibetan pharmacy) under various names; in the Chinese Bencao 酥油 (sūyóu) is the clarified-butter / Tibetan-yak-butter drug. Greco-Roman: butter (_butyrum_) was known but treated as a Scythian / barbarian food, marginal to Greco-Roman pharmacy; ghee specifically as a classical pharmacological preparation is not Greco-Roman. The Indian classical treatment is by far the most developed._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*The substance:* *clarified butter* — butter (cream-derived butterfat) heated until the water evaporates and the milk solids brown and are strained off, yielding a pure fat with characteristic nutty aroma, long shelf life, and high smoke point (~250 °C). The standard preparation from *cultured butter* (Indian _navanīta_, made from curd / yogurt rather than directly from cream) is the *classical Indian ghee*, distinct from the *direct-cream French clarified butter (beurre clarifié)* of European cuisine — the same physical product but different precursor processing.

*Latin:* No standard botanical / zoological name for ghee per se; the source is *Bos taurus indicus* (zebu) or *B. taurus taurus* (European cattle) milk-fat. The Latin pharmacy term is *butyrum* (butter, broadly) or *butyrum depuratum* (clarified butter). _Butyrum_ from Greek βούτυρον (_boúturon_) — βοῦς (_boûs_, "ox") + τυρός (_turós_, "cheese") — "ox-cheese."#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. _βούτυρον_, _βοῦς_); Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _butyrum_). The "ox-cheese" Greek folk-etymology is real and ancient — the Greeks treated butter as a barbarian / Scythian / Thracian food and used a descriptive compound for it.]

*English:* _Ghee_, direct transliteration of Hindi _घी_ (ghī), from Sanskrit _घृत_ (_ghṛta_, "sprinkled, fattened, made with ghee"; past passive participle of √_ghṛ_ "to sprinkle, to make shine / to fatten / to anoint"). The Sanskrit root √_ghṛ_ has the dual sense of *shining / brightness* and *fattening / anointing* — both consistent with the substance's appearance and function. _Ghṛta_ thus literally means "made shining / anointed."#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "ghee, n."); Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _ghṛta_, √_ghṛ_); the root sense is well documented.]

*Sanskrit:* The classical vocabulary distinguishes a *full hierarchy of dairy lipids* by source and age:
- *क्षीर (_kṣīra_)* — milk; see _milk.typ_.
- *दधि (_dadhi_)* — curd / yogurt.
- *नवनीत (_navanīta_)* — fresh butter (typically from cultured curd, the Indian method).
- *घृत (_ghṛta_)* — clarified butter — *the foundational pharmacy lipid*.
- *पुराण-घृत (_purāṇa-ghṛta_)* — *aged ghee*, 1+ years; classical preference for many applications.
- *कुम्भ-घृत (_kumbha-ghṛta_)* — pot-aged, 11+ years.
- *महाघृत (_mahā-ghṛta_)* — aged 100+ years (literal classical claim — and *some* such samples are documented in Indian temple traditions and royal collections, though most modern claims of century-aged ghee are not verified).
- The aging system progressively *increases prabhāva and pharmacological potency*, by classical doctrine.

*Chinese:* 酥油 (sūyóu, "ghee-oil") — the clarified-butter / Tibetan-yak-butter drug; treated in Bencao Gangmu and in Tibetan-medical-tradition entries. 酥 alone (sū, "ghee / clarified butter / soft cream").#footnote[Sanskrit hierarchy: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_annapānavidhi_, dairy section); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 45; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dugdha-varga_ and _Ghṛta-varga_ (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba). The aged-ghee classification is universal across the saṃhitā tradition; the 100-year claim is more aspirational than verifiable in most cases. 酥油 / 酥 in Chinese: _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Hindi:* घी (ghī).

== Classical Indian preparation method (sāmānya pāka-vidhi)

The classical Sanskrit method, given in Caraka Sūtra 27 and elaborated in Sharangadhara:
1. *Milk* → cultured to *curd* (yogurt, _dadhi_) with overnight inoculation.
2. *Curd* → churned with cool water in the traditional wooden churn (_madhana-daṇḍa_) → *butter* (_navanīta_) floats on top.
3. *Butter* → heated slowly over moderate heat; water evaporates, milk solids settle to the bottom and brown; the *clear golden liquid* is strained off through cotton cloth as *ghee*. The strained-out browned solids (_ghee-residue_) are themselves a folk-food.

The *yogurt-derived* (cultured) method is the classical Indian, distinct from the European *direct-cream* clarification. The cultured method produces *short-chain fatty acid metabolites* (butyrate, acetate) from the lactic-acid fermentation, which carry into the final ghee in trace amounts and contribute to the distinctive aroma and putative pharmacology.

*Medicated ghee preparations (Ayurvedic_ ghṛta-pāka_):* ghee is the *vehicle par excellence* for medicating with herbal decoctions, producing the canonical *medicated ghṛta* class of formulas (Triphalā-ghṛta, Phala-ghṛta, Brāhmī-ghṛta, Mahā-tikta-ghṛta, Shatāvarī-ghṛta, etc. — see relevant single-substance entries). The preparation involves cooking ghee with the herbal decoction and powder over many hours; the lipid-soluble actives transfer into the ghee, producing a *time-stable, fat-soluble-active-rich* preparation.#footnote[Preparation method: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 13 (sneha-adhyāya, on lipid preparation); Sharangadhara Saṃhitā, Madhyamakhaṇḍa, _sneha-kalpanā_ (lipid-preparation chapter); Murthy translation (Chaukhamba). Medicated-ghṛta preparation is one of the foundational Ayurvedic pharmaceutical techniques.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View — and Tibetan-Mongolian dairy pharmacy

酥油 enters Chinese pharmacy chiefly through the Tang-period absorption of Tibetan / Central Asian dairy tradition; the Bencao Gangmu treats 酥 in the animal-products section.

*Property (性):* Neutral (平) to slightly cool. *Flavor (味):* Sweet (甘). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Lung (肺), Large Intestine (大腸).

*Actions:* 補虛潤燥 (tonifies vacuity, moistens dryness), 養陰生津 (nourishes yin and generates fluids), 潤腸 (moistens intestines for constipation from dryness). Indicated for vacuity emaciation, dry cough, dryness constipation, dry skin.

*Cautions:* contraindicated in damp-heat patterns, heavy Spleen-vacuity-damp, and heavy diarrhea.

*Tibetan-Mongolian register:* yak-butter ghee is central to Tibetan and Mongolian dietary and medical tradition — the *butter tea* (酥油茶 _sūyóu chá_) is the staple drink of the Tibetan plateau, paired with tsampa (barley flour); this is the classical dietary register for the high-altitude population.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Ghṛta is one of the most-praised substances in the saṃhitās. Caraka calls it "the best of all _snehas_ (lipids)" — superior to oil, marrow, fat — for *internal medical use*.#footnote[Ghṛta as best of snehas: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 13 (sneha-adhyāya); standard classical praise.]

*Rasa:* Madhura (sweet).
*Guṇa:* Guru (heavy), Snigdha (unctuous), Mṛdu (soft), Manda (slow), Picchila (slimy), Śīta (cool — in the guṇa sense; distinct from vīrya).
*Vīrya:* *Śīta (cooling)* — *the distinguishing feature among the snehas*: ghee is *cooling*, while sesame oil (the second great Ayurvedic lipid) is warming. This makes ghee the *Pitta-pacifying* lipid and sesame oil the *Vāta-pacifying* lipid.
*Vipāka:* Madhura (sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of ghṛta: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Ghṛta-varga_; the śīta-vīrya distinction from sesame oil is foundational dravyaguna.]

*Prabhāva:* *Yogavāhī* — *carries other drugs / amplifies their action* — *the most-cited prabhāva of any Ayurvedic substance*. Ghee is *the vehicle of medication*; it both carries lipid-soluble actives into the body (the modern bioavailability-enhancement reading) and *takes on the qualities of substances cooked with it* without losing its own essential qualities (the classical yogavāhī reading). This is the foundational pharmaceutical property; medicated ghee preparations rely on it.

*Sāttvic / spiritual properties* — ghee is classed in Indian dietetics as *sāttvic* (clarity-promoting, suitable for spiritual practice); use in temple offerings, daily lamp-lighting, and ritual cooking is universal.

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies *Vāta and Pitta* (the snigdha-śīta-madhura profile); mildly Kapha-aggravating in excess (the snigdha-guru).

*Karma:*
- _Rasāyana_ — rejuvenative; especially *aged ghee* — _purāṇa-ghṛta_, considered ever more potent with age.
- _Medhya_ — promotes intellect / memory; *the Ayurvedic substance* for intellectual support, both as plain ghee in daily diet and as medicated _brāhmī-ghṛta_ etc.
- _Cakṣuṣya_ — eye-benefiting; ghee is the classical eye-lubricant, both topically (in netra-tarpana, the medicated eye-bath) and dietarily.
- _Balya, bṛṃhaṇa, jīvanīya_ — strengthening, anabolic, vitalizing.
- _Vṛṣya_ — promotes _śukra_.
- _Vāta-anulomana_ — relieves Vāta gas / constipation.
- _Vraṇa-ropaṇa_ — wound-healing (internal and external).
- _Sneha-pāna_ — the *foundational pañcakarma sneha-pāna preparation* — pre-cleansing oil-loading with progressive doses of plain or medicated ghee to lubricate the channels and prepare for purgation / emesis. Used at the start of pañcakarma to make the doṣas mobile.
- _Mūrcchita_ — properly-prepared / medicated ghee (the Sharangadhara method).
- Used in: Vāta and Pitta disorders, mental health, intellect support, eye disorders, _rasāyana_ / longevity contexts, _vraṇa-cikitsā_, pañcakarma preparation, ritual offering.

*Sneha-pāna* is one of the most-developed classical Ayurvedic clinical procedures — graduated doses of medicated ghee, taken on empty stomach, increased over 3–7 days until the body shows signs of saturation (_sneha-siddhi-lakṣaṇa_), preparing the body for therapeutic cleansing.#footnote[Karma compiled from Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 13 (sneha-adhyāya), 27 (annapānavidhi), Cikitsāsthāna throughout; Bhāvaprakāśa, _Ghṛta-varga_; Sharangadhara, _sneha-kalpanā_. Sneha-pāna procedure: Caraka Sūtrasthāna 13 and Sharangadhara, _sneha-pāna_ chapter.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S/N) Conditional:* Kapha excess, _āma_ (undigested food / metabolic toxicity), weak _agni_ — the _bṛṃhaṇa-snigdha_ character is inappropriate in these states; the rule is _āma-clearing first, then snehana_.
- *(S) Specific saṃyoga viruddha:* *ghee with cool water immediately after* (some sources); *ghee with honey in equal proportions* (a famous _saṃyoga viruddha_ — equal-by-weight ghee + honey is held to be toxic, and is universally avoided in classical pharmacy; the modern reading invokes HMF / oxidation products, similar to the cooked-honey rule — see _honey.typ_).
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Cardiovascular concerns:* ghee is ~60% saturated fat; the classical "ghee is heart-healthy" framing has been popularized in modern wellness contexts but the *clinical evidence is mixed* — modest reductions in LDL with butterfat replacing trans-fats are documented; modest LDL elevation with butterfat replacing polyunsaturated oils is also documented. The Indian observational data (high ghee + traditional diet population) shows cardiovascular outcomes comparable to or better than modern industrialized diets; the *isolated saturated-fat-from-ghee in a Westernized diet* is a different metabolic context.
  - *Lactose / casein content of ghee is very low* (the milk solids are removed in clarification), so ghee is often tolerated by lactose-intolerant and many casein-sensitive individuals — though residual amounts persist and severely allergic individuals should be cautious.
  - *Calorie density:* ~900 kcal/100 g; portion control in calorie-restricted contexts.
  - *Sourcing / adulteration:* commercial ghee may be adulterated with vegetable oils (vanaspati / hydrogenated fats, palm oil); reputable sourcing matters substantially. Pure cow-milk ghee from grass-fed cows has a different fatty-acid profile (higher CLA, vitamin K2) than feedlot-cow ghee.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Clarified butter is treated extensively in Tibb under the name_ samn _— with substantial classical pharmacology and substantial Tibb al-Nabawī attestation. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ _for the foundational framework._

*Arabic:* سمن (_samn_) — generic clarified butter / ghee; سمن بقري (_samn baqarī_) — cow's; سمن بلدي (_samn baladī_) — country-style traditional ghee. Persian: روغن (_rowghan_), specifically روغن زرد (_rowghan-e zard_, "yellow butter") for ghee. Urdu/Hakeemi: گھی (_ghī_, the Sanskrit-derived word) is the dominant Urdu form; سمن is the formal Unani.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī:* multiple hadith mention butter / ghee favorably; the *qit'a min al-samn* (piece of ghee) recommended as morning food in some traditions (Bukhari, _Kitāb al-Aṭ'imah_). The Prophetic-medicine register treats ghee as a strengthening tonic, with explicit pairing-with-honey rules.

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Moist in the 1st degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _samn_ entry; Anṭākī gives slight Hot-Moist 2nd). The *contrast with milk's Cold-Moist 1st* is the classical chemistry-of-processing reading: the cooking transforms milk's coolness into the warming-moistening register of clarified butter. This *diverges* from the Ayurvedic _śīta vīrya_ classification of ghee (Ayurveda holds ghee cooling, in distinction from sesame oil's warming character — the cross-system divergence is real and is one of the standing classical disagreements).

*Af'āl:* *Muqawwī al-rūḥ* (vital-spirit tonic); *muqawwī al-jasad* (body-strengthening); *mughadhdhī jiddan* (highly nourishing); *muraṭṭib* (humectant); *mufarriḥ* (mood-elevating in moderate dose); *muḥsin li-l-bashara* (skin-improving); *mufattiḥ ḥalq* (throat-opening, for hoarse cough); *muqawwī al-bāh* (aphrodisiac, in compound); *mubarriḍ li-l-aʿṣāb* (nerve-calming).

*Istemālāt:* convalescence; *amrāḍ al-sawdā'* (melancholic / dryness diseases); chronic dry cough; debility; postpartum recovery; pulmonary support; *amrāḍ al-aʿṣāb* (nervous diseases, with herbs); the *muqawwī al-dimāgh* (brain tonic) register, foundational for Unani nootropic compounds (parallel to Ayurvedic *medhya-ghṛta* preparations).

*Mawāne':* phlegmatic constitution in excess; weak digestion; *amrāḍ al-balgham* (phlegmatic diseases) in heavy dose; obesity; diabetes (modern).

*Muṣliḥ:* *honey* — the canonical pairing for moderating ghee's heaviness; *but in unequal proportions only* — the Unani tradition preserves the Ayurvedic equal-weight ghee-honey saṃyoga-viruddha doctrine in modified form; *rose-water* and *cardamom* as aromatic correctors.

*Badal:* sesame oil (*duhn al-simsim*) — in some indications; almond oil (*duhn al-lawz*) for nervous indications; vegetable-fat substitutes for fasting / Lenten contexts.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _samn_ entry; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*, _samn_ entry — comprehensive with cow / sheep / goat distinctions; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _ghī / samn_ entry — bridges classical Tibb with Mughal-Hakeemi practice; Ibn Qayyim, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_, _samn_ chapter.#footnote[Tibb samn: Avicenna, Ibn al-Bayṭār, Khazāyin al-Adwiya. The Mizāj divergence from Ayurvedic śīta vīrya is one of the standing cross-system disagreements — flagged.]

*Tibb formulas using ghee/samn:* *Maʿjūn-i Sāʿat al-Khayr* (electuary with ghee base for nervous tonic); *Khamīrah-i Marwārīd* (pearl-electuary, with milk-and-ghee bases); *Ḥalwā* class confections of Mughal-Hakeemi tradition; *Khichṛī* (the Indian rice-and-mung dish) treated in Unani as a balanced base-food when prepared with ghee; *Itrīfal* class preparations use ghee as part of the binding base.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese / Tibetan medicine

- *酥油茶 (Sūyóu Chá, "butter tea")* — Tibetan staple; brick-tea + yak-butter ghee + salt + tsampa-barley-flour additions; the canonical high-altitude dietary preparation.
- *Tibetan medicated butter preparations* — analogous to Ayurvedic medicated ghee; used in Tibetan medicine for similar saṃskāra purposes.
- *酥蜜煎 (Sū Mì Jiān)* — ghee-honey preparation in some Chinese pharmacy contexts; with the *Indian saṃyoga-viruddha caveat* (equal-by-weight ghee + honey is held toxic in Ayurveda — modern Chinese-medical formulations using both must observe proportionality).

=== Ayurveda

- *Sneha-pāna* — pañcakarma preparation; plain or medicated ghee, graduated doses.
- *Triphalā-ghṛta* — medicated ghee with triphala for eye disorders and detoxification.
- *Brāhmī-ghṛta* — medicated ghee with brāhmī (Bacopa monnieri) for intellect / memory / psychiatric disorders.
- *Phala-ghṛta* — medicated ghee for women's infertility.
- *Mahā-tikta-ghṛta, Mahā-pañca-ghṛta* — bitter-tonic ghees for chronic skin disease and inflammation.
- *Shatāvarī-ghṛta, Ashwagandhā-ghṛta* — see those entries.
- *Cyavanaprāśa* (see chyavanprash.typ) — uses ghee as one of two principal vehicles.
- *Khaṇḍa-kuṣmāṇḍa, Vasāvalehya* — many compound _avalehas_ use ghee as part of the base.
- *Ritual / daily use:* daily small ghee on rice / dal / chapatis — the canonical Indian dietary lipid; lamp-ghee for temple lights.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* butter was treated as a *Scythian / Thracian / barbarian* food in the Greek and Roman view (the Mediterranean tradition was olive-oil-based; butter was the "northern barbarian" lipid). _Hippocrates_ mentions butter as a topical preparation; _Dioscorides_ (II.81) treats _boúturon_ briefly. *Ghee specifically was not a Greco-Roman drug* — the clarification process is Indian.
- *Medieval European:* clarified butter is a kitchen practice in many cuisines; the *Indian / Bengali ghee tradition* enters European pharmacy and cuisine via colonial-period contact.
- *Modern Western:* a substantial recent wholefoods adoption — ghee as "the healthy butter" — driven by paleo / Indian-cooking / wellness culture; clinical evidence for the specific framing is *modest, mostly observational*.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Composition, ghee, approximate per 100 g — verify against published surveys; substantial variation by cattle diet, season, processing.

- Energy ~900 kcal
- *Fat ~99–100 g* — essentially pure fat
- Saturated fatty acids ~60% — palmitic acid, stearic acid; *butyric acid (C4:0)* is a distinctive small fraction (~4%), the short-chain fatty acid named for butter
- Monounsaturated ~30% (oleic acid)
- Polyunsaturated ~3–5% (linoleic, α-linolenic)
- *Conjugated linoleic acid (CLA)* — substantial in grass-fed-cow ghee; less in feedlot-cow
- Trans fatty acids — natural trans fatty acids (vaccenic acid) from rumen biohydrogenation; *not* the industrial trans fats associated with cardiovascular harm
- Cholesterol ~250–300 mg/100 g
- Vitamins: A, D, E, K (especially K2 in grass-fed ghee) — fat-soluble vitamins
- Very low lactose, very low casein
- Aromatic compounds: methyl ketones, lactones (notably γ-dodecalactone), and short-chain fatty acid esters from the heated milk-solids browning (Maillard / Strecker reactions)

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Bioavailability enhancement:* lipid-soluble actives (curcumin, withanolides, fat-soluble vitamins) have substantially better oral bioavailability when administered in or with ghee — the modern pharmacological vindication of the classical _yogavāhī_ property.
- *Butyrate / short-chain fatty acids:* butyric acid in ghee, and the gut-microbiome production of butyrate from ghee-supported fermentation, have been studied for colonic-epithelial health; preclinical and small clinical support.
- *Vitamin K2:* present in meaningful amounts in grass-fed ghee; relevant for vascular calcium handling and bone health.
- *Cardiovascular evidence:* mixed — classical Indian observational data favorable; isolated saturated-fat-in-Western-diet less favorable; clinical-trial evidence does not support strong "heart-protective" claims but also does not support strong "heart-harmful" claims. Population context matters.
- *Anti-inflammatory:* preclinical evidence for moderate use; the CLA fraction contributes.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the classical _yogavāhī_ + the modern bioavailability-enhancement convergence is one of the cleanest classical-modern alignments in materia medica.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary use:* ~1–2 tablespoons (~15–30 g) per day in the traditional Indian dietary register is the daily-use range; well-tolerated in most healthy adults.
- *Sneha-pāna:* graduated medicated-ghee doses of 30–250 mL/day during pañcakarma preparation, under qualified supervision only.
- *Risk groups:* (a) *significant cardiovascular disease* or *familial hypercholesterolemia* — discuss with a clinician; (b) *severe dairy allergy* — even residual ghee proteins may matter; (c) *Kapha-excess / metabolic syndrome* — moderate or substitute; (d) *active āma* — by classical doctrine, contraindicated.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

